The two fields are documented as: the `lexer` is `"The underlying source for the tokens."` and `tokens` is `"A vector containing all the tokens emitted by the lexer."` The comments note this vector holds `"both the trivia and non-trivia tokens."`

## The `do_bump` Method

```rust
fn do_bump(&mut self) {
    loop {
        let kind = self.lexer.next_token();
        if kind.is_trivia() {
            self.tokens
                .push(Token::new(kind, self.current_range(), self.current_flags()));
            continue;
        }
        break;
    }
}
```

## The `next_non_trivia_token` Method

```rust
fn next_non_trivia_token(&mut self) -> TokenKind {
    loop {
        let kind = self.lexer.next_token();
        if kind.is_trivia() {
            continue;
        }
        break kind;
    }
}
```

## The `re_lex_logical_token` Method

```rust
pub(crate) fn re_lex_logical_token(&mut self) {
    let mut non_logical_newline_start = None;
    for token in self.tokens.iter().rev() {
        match token.kind() {
            TokenKind::NonLogicalNewline => {
                non_logical_newline_start = Some(token.start());
            }
            TokenKind::Comment => continue,
            _ => break,
        }
    }

    if self.lexer.re_lex_logical_token(non_logical_newline_start) {
        let current_start = self.current_range().start();
        while self
            .tokens
            .last()
            .is_some_and(|last| last.start() >= current_start)
        {
            self.tokens.pop();
        }
    }
}
```

## How It Tracks Non-Logical Newline Positions

The method iterates backward over the collected tokens to find the starting position of a non-logical newline. When it encounters a `NonLogicalNewline` token, it records that token's start position into `non_logical_newline_start`. Comment tokens are skipped over during this reverse traversal, and any other token kind stops the loop. This tracked position is then passed to the lexer's own re-lexing routine, and if re-lexing succeeds, tokens at or after the current start position are removed from the vector.

**Difference summary:** Both loop methods pull tokens from the lexer, but `do_bump` stores trivia tokens into the vector while `next_non_trivia_token` discards trivia without recording it, returning only the resulting kind.

